    {
      "number": 3,
      "type": "msq",
      "marks": 4,
      "body": [
        { "type": "text", "md": "Consider the ER diagram below for a university enrolment system." },
        {
          "type": "er",
          "caption": "University enrolment",
          "entities": [
            {
              "name": "Student",
              "attributes": [
                { "name": "roll_no", "key": "primary" },
                { "name": "name" },
                { "name": "phone", "multivalued": true }
              ]
            },
            {
              "name": "Course",
              "attributes": [
                { "name": "course_id", "key": "primary" },
                { "name": "title" },
                { "name": "credits" }
              ]
            },
            {
              "name": "Section",
              "weak": true,
              "attributes": [
                { "name": "sec_no", "key": "partial" },
                { "name": "room" }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "relationships": [
            { "name": "Enrolls", "from": "Student", "to": "Section", "cardinality": "M:N", "total_participation": true },
            { "name": "HasSection", "from": "Course", "to": "Section", "cardinality": "1:N", "total_participation": true }
          ]
        },
        { "type": "text", "md": "Which of the following statements are correct? Select all that apply." }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "content": [{ "type": "text", "md": "`Section` is a weak entity and requires `Course` for its identity." }],
          "is_correct": true
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "content": [{ "type": "text", "md": "The `Enrolls` relationship requires a separate relation when mapped to the relational model." }],
          "is_correct": true
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "content": [{ "type": "text", "md": "`phone` can be stored as a single column in the `Student` relation without violating 1NF." }]
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "content": [{ "type": "text", "md": "Every `Student` must be enrolled in at least one `Section`." }],
          "is_correct": true
        }
      ],
      "solution": {
        "kind": "authored",
        "body": [
          { "type": "text", "md": "A is true because `Section` has only a partial key (`sec_no`) and depends on `Course`. B follows from the M:N cardinality. C is false - a multivalued attribute violates 1NF and needs its own relation. D is true because `Student` has total participation in `Enrolls` (the double line)." }
        ],
        "video_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=REPLACE_ME"
      },
      "topics": ["er-model", "weak-entities", "normalization"],
      "difficulty": "hard"
    },

    {
      "number": 4,
      "type": "programming",
      "marks": 5,
      "body": [
        { "type": "text", "md": "Given the schema below, write a SQL query returning the three highest delivery fees per city, ordered by city and then by fee descending." },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "sql",
          "filename": "schema.sql",
          "source": "CREATE TABLE Delivery_Fee (\n  order_id     INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  city         VARCHAR(30) NOT NULL,\n  distance_km  DECIMAL(5,2) NOT NULL,\n  fee          DECIMAL(6,2) NOT NULL\n);"
        }
      ],
      "correct_answer": "SELECT city, order_id, fee FROM (SELECT city, order_id, fee, RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY city ORDER BY fee DESC) AS rnk FROM Delivery_Fee) t WHERE rnk <= 3 ORDER BY city, fee DESC;",
      "solution": {
        "kind": "authored",
        "body": [
          { "type": "text", "md": "A window function is the cleanest approach - `RANK()` partitioned by city:" },
          {
            "type": "code",
            "language": "sql",
            "source": "SELECT city, order_id, fee\nFROM (\n  SELECT city, order_id, fee,\n         RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY city ORDER BY fee DESC) AS rnk\n  FROM Delivery_Fee\n) t\nWHERE rnk <= 3\nORDER BY city, fee DESC;"
          },
          { "type": "text", "md": "Note that `RANK()` keeps ties, so a city with two orders sharing the third-highest fee returns four rows. Use `ROW_NUMBER()` if you need exactly three." }
        ],
        "video_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=REPLACE_ME"
      },
      "topics": ["sql", "window-functions"],
      "difficulty": "hard"
    },

